  #line-at(72.024pt, 402.05pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 425.59pt, "We probe the evaporation mechanism, and the associated adhesion dynamics of liquid ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 446.23pt, "capillary bridges connecting two curved, solid substrates. The coupled thermo-fluid-species ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 466.99pt, "transport and the transient evolution of capillary adhesion during evaporation are ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 487.63pt, "systematically examined. An accurate, fully coupled transient numerical framework is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 508.39pt, "developed, wherein the equilibrium capillary profiles are first determined from level set ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 529.03pt, "method. Next, the evaporation is simulated via Arbitrary Lagrangian-Eulerian (ALE) ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 549.79pt, "framework to accurately track the moving liquid-vapor interface. The combined influence of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 570.43pt, "substrate curvature, surface wettability, and solid’s thermal conductivity on evaporation and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 591.22pt, "capillary adhesion character is comprehensively analysed. The simulation methodology is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 611.86pt, "robustly validated against published literature for capillary profiles, evaporation rates, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 632.62pt, "capillary forces, demonstrating good agreement. Our results reveal that the evaporation ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 653.26pt, "characteristics of both hydrophilic and superhydrophobic (SH) liquid bridges are strongly ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 674.02pt, "governed by substrate’s curvature and thermal conductivity, and increasing values pose ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 694.66pt, "favourable condition for augmented interfacial mass transfer rate. The innately non-uniform ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 715.42pt, "vapour flux generates spatially varying evaporative cooling, producing surface-tension ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 736.06pt, "gradients that drive internal thermo-capillary circulation. A non-dimensional scaling analysis ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 756.84pt, "shows that Marangoni flow dominates buoyancy-induced flow throughout. Also, increasing ")
]

