#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.91pt, 790.2pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 83.18pt, "tension, overall radius of curvature, Laplace pressure, and gravitational effects. Here, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.82pt, "geometrical parameter ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(183.53pt, 103.82pt, "R")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(190.97pt, 104.78pt, "0")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(195.05pt, 103.82pt, " takes into account both gorge and external radius of curvature; and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.58pt, "thereby is strongly dictated by the wetting properties of solid surface and solid-liquid bridge ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 145.22pt, "geometry [13]. For instance, between superhydrophobic (SH) surfaces, both external and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 165.98pt, "gorge profile becomes convex, thereby resulting in a nodoid-shaped (a hyperbolic roulette) ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.62pt, "bridge; whereas between hydrophilic surfaces a concave gorge and concave external profile ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.38pt, "lead to an unduloidal (an elliptic roulette) bridge profile [14]. Both these profiles, under ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 228.02pt, "respective positive and negative internal pressure conditions, were experimentally realized by ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.81pt, "Mielniczuk et al [15] under negligible gravity conditions. In presence of gravitational effects, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.45pt, "the conventional analytical models cannot be employed to solve Young-Laplace equation due ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 290.21pt, "to redundant boundary conditions, and in such cases numerical shooting techniques are ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.85pt, "needed [16]. Also, the capillary profile also depends on the surface tension at the liquid-gas ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.61pt, "interface, which in turn is governed by the thermal state of the system. For most liquids, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.25pt, "surface tension decreases with increasing temperature. Consequently, variations in the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 373.01pt, "thermal state (such as arising from external heating or evaporative cooling) can significantly ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 393.65pt, "alter the equilibrium capillary profile. ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 422.35pt, "The temporal evolution of capillary profile significantly affects the evaporative ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 443.11pt, "transport due to the changing interfacial curvature; as local variation in the curvature of the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 463.75pt, "liquid-vapor interface governs the local vapor concentration gradient in the gaseous domain ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 484.51pt, "[17]. The solid-liquid-contact geometry, thermo-physical properties, thermal effects, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 505.15pt, "ambient conditions [18-21] also govern the capillary evolution. For instance, a larger bridge ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 525.91pt, "height results in an enhanced evaporative mass loss rate due to reduced confinement effects ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 546.55pt, "[20]. Also, a higher substrate temperature or lower ambient relative humidity substantially ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 567.31pt, "enhances the evaporation rate, as reported in recent studies [18, 19]. These parameters also ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 587.98pt, "markedly influence the convective transport phenomena within the liquid bridge. The non-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 608.74pt, "uniform evaporative mass flux [22] induces uneven evaporative cooling, thereby generating ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 629.38pt, "internal flow through thermal Marangoni convection or buoyancy-driven advection, similar ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 650.14pt, "to non-isothermal capillary bridges [23]. Furthermore, confinement effects may significantly ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 670.78pt, "modify the internal advection dynamics through changes in the evaporation rate, similar to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 691.54pt, "sessile droplets [24]. Also, in the presence of external confinement, vortex-induced ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 712.18pt, "modifications of the gaseous flow field may further alter the evaporation rate [25]. Therefore, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 732.94pt, "the advection characteristics in both the liquid and gaseous domains play a crucial role in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 753.6pt, "determining the overall stability and evaporation behavior of capillary bridges. ")
]

// arXiv source page 4
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.91pt, 790.2pt, "4 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(108.02pt, 83.18pt, "Moreover, the capillary force in a liquid bridge arises from the combined ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.82pt, "contributions of surface tension and Laplace pressure and is therefore strongly governed by ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.58pt, "the bridge geometry and wetting characteristics [26, 27]. For a given bridge volume, a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 145.22pt, "smaller separation distance increases the interfacial curvature, thereby enhancing both the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 165.98pt, "Laplace pressure and surface tension contributions acting between the substrates [28]. Also, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.62pt, "the wettability of the confining surfaces strongly influences the contact angle and contact ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.38pt, "radius, resulting in predominantly attractive (suction) and repulsive capillary forces for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 228.02pt, "hydrophilic and superhydrophobic (SH) surfaces, respectively [17]. Consequently, the bridge ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.81pt, "geometry and wetting state play a pivotal role in determining the magnitude and direction of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.45pt, "the capillary force. During evaporation, continuous interfacial mass loss alters the transient ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 290.21pt, "capillary profile and its characteristic geometrical features, such as the gorge and external ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.85pt, "radii of curvature. These geometric features are further affected by the contact line dynamics ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.61pt, "[29] which in turn is dictated by the wetting state and curvature of the solid substrates similar ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.25pt, "to sessile droplets [30]. As a result, during evaporation, the capillary force initially increases ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 373.01pt, "to a maximum until the onset of pressure instability, beyond which it progressively decreases ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 393.65pt, "and eventually becomes repulsive (positive gauge pressure) at bridge rupture [28, 29]. Such ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 414.41pt, "transient variations in capillary force govern particle rearrangement and self-assembly, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 435.07pt, "enabling the fabrication of ordered micro- and nanostructures for advanced functional ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 455.83pt, "materials and coatings.")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 484.51pt, "The foregoing literature highlights the influential role of solid-liquid geometry in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 505.15pt, "governing the capillary profile and evaporation behavior of liquid bridges under varied ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 525.91pt, "conditions. It also demonstrates the significant effects of surface wettability and the thermo-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 546.55pt, "physical properties of the solid and liquid phases on the internal thermo-fluidic transport and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 567.31pt, "capillary force dynamics. However, in practical applications, solid substrates are seldom ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 587.98pt, "perfectly planar, and curved geometries are more commonly encountered. Such substrate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 608.74pt, "curvature, together with the wetting state, can substantially modify the equilibrium capillary ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 629.38pt, "profile by altering the minimum-energy configuration of the whole system. Furthermore, in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 650.14pt, "microgravity experiments involving liquid bridges, the tip material and consequently the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 670.78pt, "thermal conductivity of the confining substrates can vary over a wide range, thereby ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 691.54pt, "influencing the evaporation process.  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 720.22pt, "Despite their practical significance, only a few studies [28, 29, 31] have considered ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 740.86pt, "the influence of substrate curvature on the evaporation behavior of liquid bridges. Moreover, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 761.64pt, "the coupled effects of evaporative cooling, thermo-fluidic transport, and transient adhesion ")
]

