#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.91pt, 790.2pt, "5 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 83.18pt, "dynamics have remained largely unexplored. To address these gaps, the present study ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.82pt, "numerically investigates the coupled thermo-fluid-adhesion dynamics of evaporating liquid ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.58pt, "bridges. For this purpose, the capillary profile is numerically obtained using level-set method, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 145.22pt, "and the evaporative transport phenomena of liquid bridges are subsequently modeled within ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 165.98pt, "an Arbitrary Lagrangian-Eulerian (ALE) framework. The coupled effects of substrate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.62pt, "curvature under different wettability and thermal conductivity conditions are explored ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.38pt, "systematically. The findings provide fundamental insights for optimizing evaporation-driven ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 228.02pt, "particle assembly and additive manufacturing processes, where the transient evolution of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.81pt, "liquid-bridge geometry and capillary forces governs particle packing, structural integrity, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.45pt, "the resulting microstructure. Furthermore, the mechanistic understanding of evaporation-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 290.21pt, "driven adhesion dynamics developed in the present study provides a scientific basis for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.85pt, "improving the quantification of capillary adhesion, tip-sample interaction forces, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.61pt, "nanoscale interfacial phenomena in atomic force microscopy (AFM) across a broad range of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.25pt, "operating conditions [32, 33]. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 14.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 408.29pt, "2. Physical system, mathematical description, and computer simulations ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 431.71pt, "We consider the evaporation phenomenon of a capillary bridge between the curved faces of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 452.47pt, "two adjacent cylindrical substrates in an initially quiescent and isothermal environment. The ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 473.11pt, "problem involves coupled mass, momentum, and energy transport between the adjacent solid, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 493.87pt, "liquid and gaseous domains. At initial conditions, the liquid molecules being saturated at the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 514.51pt, "liquid-vapor interface diffuse to the surrounding gaseous domain solely due to concentration ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 535.27pt, "difference. During this process, latent heat is extracted from the interface, resulting in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 555.91pt, "evaporative cooling and the development of temperature gradients within both the liquid and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 576.67pt, "gaseous phases. These thermal disturbances further propagate into the adjacent solid domains ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 597.34pt, "through heat conduction. This thermal non-uniformity induces bulk motion in both fluid and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 618.1pt, "gaseous domain through thermal Marangoni effects or buoyancy driven advection. Such bulk ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 638.74pt, "fluid motion may result in internal heat advection thereby the affecting the saturation ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 659.5pt, "concentration of vapor at the liquid-vapor interface. In other words, the evaporation effects ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 680.14pt, "i.e., the evaporative cooling and resulting bulk fluid motion directly influences its driving ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 700.9pt, "mechanism i.e., the interfacial vapor diffusion. As a result, the mass, momentum and energy ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 721.54pt, "transport processes become strongly coupled and inherently nonlinear. These phenomena ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 742.296pt, "may further be affected by the system geometry and wetting effects which in turn is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 762.96pt, "influenced by the thermo-physical properties of participating domains. ")
]

