  #line-at(72.024pt, 83.18pt, "transfer rate from solid to fluid domain. Whereas, the evaporation rate is increased due to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.82pt, "altered liquid-vapor interfacial geometry. Both these factors favor steep thermal gradient in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.58pt, "solid domain to compensate for enhanced evaporative cooling effects. As a result, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 145.22pt, "temperature drop in solid domain is steeply increased than the liquid one for curved ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 165.98pt, "geometries of solid substrate. The slower increase in the temperature drop within the liquid ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.62pt, "domain also explains the corresponding reduction in the rate of increase of the velocity scale ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.38pt, "as shown in Fig. 6 (b) and 7 (b). ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 234.02pt, "For substrates with altered thermal conductivities, the variation in temperature drops ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 254.81pt, "across the solid and liquid domains differs significantly, as shown in Figs. 6(c) and 7(c). ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 275.45pt, "Specifically, the overall temperature drop increases sharply with decreasing thermal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 296.21pt, "conductivity of the solid substrate, whereas the temperature drop within the liquid domain ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 316.85pt, "decreases slightly. Consequently, although the overall evaporative cooling becomes ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 337.61pt, "substantially stronger for substrates with lower thermal conductivity, the corresponding ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 358.25pt, "velocity scale is reduced. Apparently, this trend appears counterintuitive, since stronger ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 379.01pt, "evaporative cooling is generally expected to enhance the Marangoni effects and thereby ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 399.65pt, "upscaling the flow velocity. This seemingly contradictory behavior is primarily attributed to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 420.43pt, "the limited heat conduction capability of thermally insulating substrates. Due to their poor ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 441.07pt, "thermal conductivity, a major portion of the evaporative cooling remains confined within the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 461.83pt, "solid domain rather than being transmitted to the liquid. For example, for substrates with very ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 482.47pt, "low thermal conductivity (e.g., acrylic plastic, whose thermal conductivity is even lower than ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 503.23pt, "that of water), nearly 98% of the overall temperature drop remains concentrated within the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 523.87pt, "solid domain. As a result, despite the substantial increase in the overall evaporative cooling, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 544.63pt, "the temperature drop across the liquid domain is slightly reduced. This condition ultimately ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 565.27pt, "weakens the thermal gradient within the liquid thereby suppressing the Marangoni stresses, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 586.06pt, "leading to a reduction in the characteristic flow velocity as seen in fig 6(b) and 7(b). ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
