// arXiv source page 28
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-28.png", width: 4.5pt), 72.5pt, 280pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.13pt, 790.2pt, "28 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 14.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 85.1pt, "4. Conclusions ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 113.3pt, "To conclude, we probe the thermo-hydrodynamics, species transport, and adhesion dynamics ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 134.06pt, "of evaporating capillary bridges under the combined effects of substrate curvature, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 154.7pt, "wettability, and thermal conductivity. Equilibrium capillary bridge profiles are first obtained ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 175.46pt, "using the level-set method and subsequently employed as the initial configuration for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 196.1pt, "evaporation simulations based on a transient, fully coupled Arbitrary Lagrangian-Eulerian ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 216.86pt, "(ALE) framework. The numerical predictions show good agreement with available ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 237.5pt, "experimental and theoretical studies, demonstrating the accuracy of the proposed model. The ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 258.29pt, "key findings of the present investigation are summarized as: ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 285.89pt, "For both hydrophilic and superhydrophobic (SH) surfaces, increasing the convexity of ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 306.53pt, "adjacent substrates significantly enlarges the liquid-vapor interfacial area and its radius of ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 327.17pt, "curvature, thereby enhancing the evaporative mass loss owing to the increased mass ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 347.93pt, "transfer area and steeper vapor concentration gradient at the interface. In contrast, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 368.57pt, "substrates with lower thermal conductivity suppress the evaporation rate by limiting heat ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 389.33pt, "conduction to the liquid bridge, resulting in lower interfacial temperatures and a ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 409.97pt, "consequent reduction in the local saturation vapor concentration at the interface. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 437.71pt, "The presence of solid boundary dictates diverging evaporative flux near the three-phase ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 458.23pt, "contact line. This non-uniformity in mass flux results in uneven evaporative cooling ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 478.99pt, "effects thereby set off bulk internal motion. The non-dimensional scaling analysis showed ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 499.63pt, "that the Marangoni effects strongly dominate over buoyancy effects in the overall flow ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 520.39pt, "features. This Marangoni-induced interfacial flow sweeps liquids from the contact line ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 541.03pt, "towards the gorge region thereby resulting in formation of two counter-acting vortices at ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 561.79pt, "the stable state. Moreover, this bulk internal flow causes heat advection thereby affecting ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 582.43pt, "the overall thermo-fluid-species transport phenomena during the evaporation process. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 610.18pt, "The evaporation induced velocity scales are significantly higher for curved substrates due ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 630.7pt, "to enhanced evaporative cooling caused by higher evaporation rate. However, the velocity ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 651.46pt, "scales are diminished for liquid bridges formed between low thermally conductive ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 672.1pt, "surfaces despite substantially higher evaporative cooling effects. This behavior is ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 692.86pt, "attributed to the low thermal conductance of the solid substrate, which confines nearly ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 713.5pt, "98% of the total evaporation-induced temperature drop within the substrate. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 734.26pt, "Consequently, the thermal gradient within the liquid domain is substantially weakened, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 754.92pt, "thereby suppressing the Marangoni stresses and the associated flow velocities. ")
]

